From: ADIOS scheduler analysis
Subject: [PATCH] adios: v3.3.1-SAGA -- fixes from independent ChatGPT
 code review of 0002 (verified against source before applying)

Applies ON TOP of adios-android14-6.1-v3.2.0.patch. Same base as
v3.3.0-SAGA (parts 1-6 + all prior fixes -- see commit history). The
user ran an independent deep review of the 0002 patch through ChatGPT
(two passes) and asked for each claim to be checked against the real
code, not taken on faith. Verified every claim against source; six
were confirmed real and fixed here, several others were valid-but-
lower-priority (documented, deferred) or judged incorrect/overstated
(explained, not applied). See per-item notes below.

CONFIRMED AND FIXED:

1. Race condition in adapt_depth_controller()'s window evaluation.
   Multiple CPUs completing I/O near-simultaneously could both pass
   the "total >= window_samples" check and both evaluate+reset the
   same optype's window -- not a memory-safety bug (atomics stay
   individually consistent) but a logical one: one batch of samples
   could get double-applied to the multiplier. Plausibly explains
   part of the real volatility measured empirically this session
   (randwrite swinging 9-59 MB/s between rounds). Fixed with a
   cmpxchg-based single-owner guard (window_evaluating[optype]) around
   the evaluate+reset block; the lock-free increment path is
   untouched.

   Also added, same review, same empirical motivation: a cooldown
   (default 1000ms, lm_adapt_cooldown_ms) between multiplier
   adjustments, and asymmetric step size (default +5% up /
   lm_adapt_step_permille, -10% down / lm_adapt_step_down_permille,
   was symmetric +-10%) -- standard control-theory technique to avoid
   overshoot/oscillation, directly motivated by the same volatility.

2. model_is_confident() only ever checked small_count, meaning any
   workload dominated by large I/O (file copies, APK installs,
   sequential restores) could pass thousands of large-bucket
   completions through the model and still never be "confident" --
   large_count didn't even exist as a field. Added large_count to
   latency_model_params (incremented alongside large_sum_delay/
   large_sum_bsize in lm_update_large_buckets, sideload_latency_model,
   and force_shrink_model, mirroring small_count's existing handling
   exactly). model_is_confident() now takes the request's block_size
   and checks whichever bucket (small/large) it actually belongs to --
   same size check latency_model_predict already uses.

3. The `latency` variable in adios_completed_request (upstream ADIOS's
   own metric, NOT something this patch introduced) is the gap since
   the last completion on that same CPU, not strictly this request's
   own service time -- under continuous back-to-back load it's closer
   to "inter-completion gap in a saturated pipeline." That's plausibly
   intentional for ADIOS's own regression model, so it and its use in
   latency_model_input() are completely untouched. But #3/#6 (added in
   this patch series) interpret deviation from prediction as "did
   service get worse", and under this metric a change in CONCURRENCY
   (fewer in-flight requests) can look identical to real hardware
   degradation. #3/#6 now use a separate `true_latency` (now -
   rq->io_start_time_ns, unambiguous per-request elapsed time)
   instead, computed once since io_start_time_ns is already confirmed
   non-zero by the existing early-return check above it.

4. Interactive-boost (#5) now excludes kernel threads
   (current->flags & PF_KTHREAD) -- a kworker doing I/O on behalf of
   some subsystem is not "user interaction" and boosting it fights the
   whole point of the heuristic.

5. effective_batch_limit() (u32 * u32) and #2's scale_permille
   calculation (q->nr_requests * 1000) now cast to u64 before
   multiplying. Realistically never overflows on any real device
   (batch_limit/nr_requests are always small in practice), but free to
   do correctly.

6. lm_adapt_good_ratio_pct/lm_adapt_bad_ratio_pct and
   lm_adapt_min_permille/lm_adapt_max_permille sysfs setters now
   cross-validate against each other (good < bad; min <= max) instead
   of only bounding each independently -- previously e.g. good=150/
   bad=110 (overlapping classification, order-dependent behavior) or
   min=2000/max=1000 (inverted clamp range) were both silently
   accepted.

VALID, LOWER PRIORITY, DEFERRED (not applied this revision):
- READ_ONCE/WRITE_ONCE on hot-path-read tunables: real from a strict
  KCSAN/memory-model standpoint, but aligned u32/u64 reads/writes don't
  tear on ARM64 in practice, and SAGA-Build doesn't run KCSAN. Low
  value for the risk of touching every tunable read site right now.
- 16-slot interactive-boost table -> hash table: valid scaling concern
  for real Android (many concurrent TGIDs), but the mechanism is
  already documented as best-effort (collision = occasional missed/
  wrong boost, never a correctness issue) -- a hash+probing redesign
  is more invasive than the fix is urgent.
- force_shrink_model() called directly from the completion path
  (allocates under a spinlock, GFP_ATOMIC): valid architectural point
  (a deferred-work/timer handoff would be cleaner, and the existing
  update_timer_callback() infrastructure could host it), but it only
  fires rarely by design (after lm_overrun_trigger_count consecutive
  overruns, default 20) -- deferred for a future revision rather than
  rushed into this one.
- Struct reorganization (nesting the adaptive-controller/interactive-
  boost fields into their own sub-structs instead of flat in
  adios_data): pure maintainability improvement, zero behavioral
  effect, skipped for now.
- nr_requests being called "hardware queue depth" in earlier revisions'
  comments: technically imprecise (it's the block-layer scheduler tag
  depth, not literally hctx capacity) -- comment wording was already
  fairly hedged in most places; the mechanism itself (a portable,
  always-available scaling signal) is intentional and unchanged.

JUDGED INCORRECT / NOT APPLIED, with reasoning:
- "Remove first-I/O-always-boosts behavior": the review's own example
  is a kworker's first read, which the kernel-thread exclusion above
  already fixes more precisely. Removing the fallback entirely would
  also stop boosting a freshly-launched foreground app's very first
  read (cold start) -- arguably the single most latency-sensitive real
  UX moment this mechanism exists for. Kept the fallback, fixed the
  actual overlooked case (kthreads) instead.
- "Lower the multiplier ceiling to 1.5x initially, test before 2.0x":
  reasonable caution in the abstract, but this session already has
  real 5-round benchmark data at the 2.0x ceiling showing ADIOS ahead
  in 3 of 4 metrics against Kyber -- lowering it now would contradict
  actual measured results without new evidence prompting it. The
  cooldown/asymmetric-step fix above should reduce runaway-to-ceiling
  behavior on its own regardless.

NOT submitted upstream. NOT compiled/tested against a real
android14-6.1 tree yet. All fixes were verified against the actual
source in this session (not taken on the review's word), same standard
as every patch before this one in this series.
